Scenario/trend 1: North American macroeconomic stability, but increased short-term volatility
In the United States, real GDP grew by 2.5 per year over year in 2023 and by about 2.8 per cent in 2024; (b) Disturbed by tariff shocks and the state of federal government phasing out in 2025, the four-quarter growth rate has been weak; In June 2026, the Federal Reserve's latest forecast adjusted the median GDP growth rate for 2026-2028 to 2.2 per cent, 2.3 per cent and 2.2 per cent; Canada ' s economic growth decelerated to 1.0-1.5 per cent in 2025, and the estimate temperature rebound in 2026;
Scenario/trend 2: Four consecutive years after the downfall of the North American construction equipment Market, it is expected to reach a peak in 2026
Since the peak of overseas demand in 2021, the North American construction equipment market has been declining for four consecutive years (2022-2025) as a result of the Fed ' s interest-added cycle; According to AEM November 2025, the rental market for building equipment grew at a rate of about 3.33 per cent (or 3.7 per cent in 2027) and residential upswings to 1.8 per cent in the year 2026, with capital growth slightly slower than previously; As the Fed ' s interest-reduction cycle moves forward, North America ' s demand is expected to match domestic markets into a new up-to-date cycle;
Event/trend 3: Continued expansion of U.S. equipment
The American Leasing Association (ARA) latest forcast: the size of the construction and industrial equipment (CIE) plus utility rental industry increased by 3.6 per cent in 2026 to $83.5 billion, up 2.8 per cent from the previous level; (b) Continuous pressure on procurement costs in an environment of high interest rates on a “rent-for-purchase” basis;
Scenarios/trend 4: Disaggregated industries are still divided, mining is relatively strong and property continues to be under pressure
(a) The prices of minerals (copper, gold, etc.) have remained historically high, capital expenditures in the mining sector have remained strong and demand for large tons of equipment has been relatively strong; Residential and commercial property in the United States continues to hover at the bottom against a background of lower interest rates, but still relatively high; Public investment in infrastructure has continued to grow steadily.
Reciprocity/trend 5: interest rates go down the road, and tariff escalation of inflation remains variable
(a) The Federal Reserve has been able to reduce interest rates by 75 basis points since September 2025, and the federal fund rate has fallen to 3.5 - 3.75 per cent; In January 2026, FOMC suspended interest reduction and considered the policy to be at a “moderate and neutral” level; In the event of tariff effects, PPE inflation is expected to rise to 3.6 per cent by the end of 2026, and the game between inflation and interest-reduction rhythms remains a key variable affecting equipment procurement costs;
Symptom 6: Continued tariff escalation of manufacturing and import costs and disruption of supply chains
The United States manufacturing industry PMI has experienced several consecutive months of contraction through tariff shocks, with the mainframe plant generally reflecting delays in delivery, cost escalation and some project extensions or cancellations; Enterprises generally adopt a “cost transfer + efficiency reduction” strategy to address tariff normality.

Indicator/Trend 1: COREPLAND, CA, continues to advance, but federal clean energy support has contracted significantly.
COREplanned itself is continuing: California has provided a total additional $70 million for the financial year 2025-2027, and has reopened its claim in the third quarter of 2026 to continue to encourage the purchase of zero-emission non-road equipment with subsidies;
However, at the federal level, there has been a shift: the “Great America” Act, which came into effect in July 2025, substantially abolished the tax credit for clean energy production and investment under IRAs; the federal financial support for electric equipment has decreased significantly; the national commercialization of new energy engineering machinery has slowed; and California has become a minority that continues to be promoted.
Event/trend 2: Increased federal-Californian emissions regulatory rights game and increased policy uncertainty
In February 2026, EPA finally abolished the 2009 Hazard Determination, which is the regulatory basis for greenhouse gases; (b) The repeal and consideration of the removal of a total of seven clean air emission exemptions in California by Congress on two occasions in 2025-2026 using the Congressional Review Act, which has been prosecuted in California (California v. EPA), but has not yet been finalized;
At the same time, California did not abandon its route: in February 2026 CARB relaunched a new non-road engineTier 5 emissions legislation that suspended for about one year. (a) The likely continuation of the two-track regulatory pattern over the next 2-3 years “Federal easing, California tightening”, with product compliance strategies requiring a combination of two standard lines;
Phenomenals/trend 3: Hazard materials
Equipment exported to the United estimate is subject to continuous compliance with hazardous waste and pollution liability regulations such as RCRA, CERCLA; As the proportion of components such as batteries, electrical systems and so on increases, relevant hazardous material compliance declarations and supply chain traceability requirements are further refined.
phenomena/trend 4: Environmental and climate adaptation aspects
The vastness of the United estimate and its diverse climatic and geographical conditions, covering a wide range of extreme environments such as extreme cold and high heat, require more adaptable engineering machinery; Users choose to install equipment with different needs, with anti-freeze devices in extreme cold areas and increased heat radiance in high hot areas.

